{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D022* decisions/D029* decisions/D031* decisions/D034* decisions/D035* decisions/D039* decisions/D040*'","aggregated_output":"# D022 — D020 completed + matched static layer offload vs engine LRU expert cache (x86-main, c2-resume)\nClaim category: runtime-placement/offload trade-off between two EXISTING llama.cpp 31288c9c6 features. Not an engine-code gain.\nConditions: image llamacpp-b0 (sha256:cf383f82...), mixE4K (sha256 2e520c4a...; hashed by orchestrator op, lab spec now names mixE4K), chat prompt, -n 160, -c 4096, temp 0, seed 1, --no-warmup, -ngl 99; one GPU job; host-load monitor: no compiler active (artifacts/x2/*hostload.log; first monitor self-matched its own pattern, verified + fixed; Codex O's objects all predate the D020 window).\n1. D020 rerun: job-8df3c7bb27 att-2 completed 10/10 under the T-M runner (2cf2925); st_prose_n18 flagged invalid_utf8 (the original crash site) and still scored (completion parser policy).\n2. Matched job job-ad883810b3 (lab@2cf2925, 9 configs x 3 rounds, order alternated per round). Medians of decode t/s [3 samples]:\n   static n24 742 MiB 128 | n18 902 MiB 152 | n12 1064 MiB 197 | n6 1226 MiB 323 | n0 1388 MiB 684\n   moe-cache c128 870 MiB 54 | c256 998 MiB 99 | c384 1126 MiB 187 | c640 1382 MiB 458\n   At every matched footprint static layer offload is faster (≈2.8x at ~880 MiB, ≈2x at ~1030, ≈1.7x at ~1180, 1.5x at ~1385). Output text identical (stdout sha256 equal) across all configs.\n3. Interpretation (consistent with D019, not proven by it): LRU-with-admission pays H2D on every miss; layer offload pays none. Neither is expert-granular AND admission-free -> T-O static hot-expert candidate is the untested quadrant.\nLimits: one prompt, decode only (prefill not compared), single host, discrete-only (PCIe). 3 samples/config.\n# D029 — C1 q8_reuse ACCEPTED: first full-engine computation-preserving gain (+2.1% Q8_0 decode), x86-main\nProtocol D026 (frozen, commit 474be3b before results). Job job-34c09bec98 (image llamacpp-q8reuse2, run_ref sha256:bd78b473..., config 111ddae8...; patch 9943dee4...), same binary GGML_CUDA_Q8_REUSE=0 vs 1, 2 ABBA cycles, 36/36 clean, host monitor 38 samples no compiler, load 2.7-5.0.\nllama-bench tg128 median t/s [range]: Q8_0 OFF 791.3 [789-792] ON 807.6 [806-808] = +2.06% (criterion >= +2%: MET, ranges disjoint). mixE4K OFF 702.9 ON 715.4 = +1.78% (below 2% -> mixE4K track \"consistent but sub-threshold\").\nllama-completion decode (chat, 160 tok): Q8_0 758.4 -> 777.0 (+2.45%); mixE4K 677.6 -> 691.2 (+2.0%). Prefill unchanged within noise.\nCorrectness: CUDA test-backend-ops MUL_MAT 2445/2445 OK; greedy text sha identical base/OFF/ON (Q8_0 65894776, mixE4K 950b46b1); PPL identical (Q8_0 9.653, mixE4K 9.9806, -ub 1).\nClassification: full-engine code gain, computation-preserving, discrete RTX 5070 Ti, Granite, batch-1 decode. Small: it's a launch/redundancy cleanup (D011 predicted 3-4.5%). Not memory-reducing; stacks with every memory technique. Component CC: cc/q8_reuse (integration: env switch, CUDA mmvq path, applies to 31288c9c6).\n# D031 — Why the hybrid split loses on Granite (nsys, T-PROF) and the proxy-scale bias it reveals\nEvidence: artifacts/codexPROF/ANALYSIS.md, cc/nsys_decode/analyze_nsys.py (new profiling CC), artifacts/x4-hs/prof/{split,static}-n12.sqlite (graph-level, same binary, 154 decode tokens after 5 warmup).\nPer token: static n12 5.35 ms (13 graph launches, 110 syncs, GPU busy 1.06 ms = GPU idle ~80%); split n12 7.38 ms (25 graph launches, 194 syncs, GPU busy 2.55 ms). Extra 2.03 ms = 1.45 ms extra GPU graph work (per-slot hot dispatch: 1535 vs 583 kernels in first decode) + 0.37 ms exposed sync/transfers + 0.21 ms host. CUDA graphs ARE reused (no capture loss).\nInterpretation (orchestrator): at batch 1 on this host, decode is dominated by per-layer CPU<->GPU handoff latency, not by expert bytes or GPU compute. The split computes FEWER CPU experts per token (~52 vs 96 at ~73% hot coverage) yet is slower because it pays the handoff in 24 layers instead of 12. T-PROF's best redesign (batched CUDA dispatch with skip IDs, -0.7..-1.1 ms) would only reach ~parity with static.\nTransferability (key factory lesson): Granite experts are 0.84 MiB; the target (~500 GB MoE) has experts ~10-50x larger, so per-layer fixed costs amortize and expert-granular placement (hot GPU / cold CPU, Strata-style) should regain its advantage. Granite-only results systematically favour layer-granular placement. => The factory needs a second proxy that genuinely exceeds VRAM with larger experts before deciding placement granularity; Granite results on placement are labelled \"small-expert regime\".\nMethodology rule: report per-layer fixed cost (handoff latency) alongside per-expert cost; predict crossover expert size = fixed_cost / per_byte_cost before choosing granularity.\n# D034 — Planner (cc/moe_planner, T-PL) predictions validated on GPU (900 MiB budget, ctx 4096)\nProtocol D032(B). Job job-56115e2bf1 (20/20 clean). 5 plans mixE4K --n-cpu-moe 19..23: GPU MiB predicted vs measured within +0.1..+0.3% (all); decode predicted vs measured +2.6..+7.2% (all optimistic; 3 samples each). Within the planner's own leave-one-out bound (16%) -> VALIDATED IN RANGE. KLD 0.040 / top-1 90.5% for all (placement does not change quality, as expected for identical weights).\nNext: add these 5 points to calibration.json (systematic optimism ~5%); validate out of range (Qwen3-30B-A3B proxy, D031) where the planner must say \"extrapolated\".\nMethod lesson: \"plan -> emitted lab tests -> measured -> prediction error\" is the factory's self-check loop; it caught a consistent decode bias on its first run.\n# D035 — Placement-aware precision (PAP) + imatrix: results (protocol D032-A), job job-3d2fbbd061 (48/48 clean)\nConditions: llamacpp-b0, chat -n 160 -c 4096, 3 alternating rounds; host monitor: no compiler, BUT the 18.6 GB proxy-2 download ran throughout (dl=3) and round 3 was slower for several configs -> decode spread 10-15% within a config (vs 1-3% in D022/D029). Speed conclusions are therefore PROVISIONAL.\nQUALITY (deterministic, 20x512 wikitext-test vs bf16 logits; solid):\n  mixE4K KLD 0.040 top1 90.5 | mix4_imat (same types/bytes, imatrix from wikitext TRAIN) 0.027 / 91.9 (-33% KLD, free) |\n  pap12_q3 0.073 / 87.3 | pap18_q3 0.086 / 85.8 | pap24_q3 0.117 / 83.6 vs pap24_q3_noimat 0.135 / 81.8 (imatrix -13%) |\n  pap12_q2 0.193 | pap18_q2 0.258 | pap24_q2 0.359 (2-bit experts unacceptable even with imatrix and only in CPU layers).\nSPEED (median decode t/s, same K vs mixE4K): pap12_q3 214.9 vs 195.7 (+9.8%), pap18_q3 +5.5%, pap24_q3 +8.7%, q2 variants +14..19%; mix4_imat vs mixE4K -3..+4% (identical bytes => file-level noise floor in this job ~±5%).\nGPU MiB unchanged (PAP only touches CPU-resident layers); host cgroup peak unchanged (weights are mmap page cache, not charged) — PAP's memory effect is on file/page-cache size: pap12_q3 738 MB vs mixE4K 816 MB (-9.6%).\nVerdict vs frozen rule (>= +5% AND KLD <= 0.08): pap12_q3 PROVISIONALLY ACCEPTED (speed needs a quiet-host 5-round rerun); all others rejected (quality). Imatrix: ACCEPTED as a factory default — same bytes, -33% KLD at 4-bit, -13% at 3-bit (calibration on wikitext-train; generalization beyond wikitext not tested).\nFactory rules: (1) always quantize experts with an imatrix from a held-out calibration set; (2) lower-bit only for CPU-resident layers and only to 3-bit; (3) speed gain from PAP is bounded by the CPU share of decode time — it matters most where the CPU part dominates (big offloaded models).\n# D039 — Proxy-2 Qwen3-30B-A3B frontier (protocol D038), job job-9f50eae42a 27/27 clean, host quiet (84 monitor samples, no compiler/download)\nImage release-1. Decode t/s median [GPU delta MiB]: K=8 93.5 [15684] | 12 73.3 [14290] | 18 54.6 [12246] | 24 43.6 [10206] | 30 36.6 [8162] | 36 31.7 [6120] | 42 27.7 [4074] | 48 24.4 [1798]. Prefill 160 -> 54 t/s. Elapsed per run (load from page cache + 128 tok): 7.7 -> 11.1 s. K=18 + FA + q8_0 KV: 12066 MiB (-180), 53.9 t/s (-1.3%).\nMemory-fit result: 10x less GPU memory (17.7 GB model -> 1.8 GB resident) for 3.8x slower decode, at unchanged weights (accuracy = Q4_K_M; placement-only).\nModel: 1/decode = t_gpu + K x c; c = (1/24.4 - 1/93.5)/40 = 0.757 ms per CPU layer = 8 experts x 2.72 MiB / 0.757 ms = ~30 GB/s effective CPU expert bandwidth (Granite D022: ~26 GB/s). TRANSFERABLE CONSTANT for this host: CPU expert path is DRAM-bandwidth bound at ~26-30 GB/s for Q4_K.\nPre-registered predictions scored: orchestrator (bytes / 26 GB/s): K=18 50-60 -> 54.6 OK; K=48 20-25 -> 24.4 OK. Planner (extrapolated from Granite per-layer calibration): K=18 8.1 vs 54.6 (-85%), K=30 4.9 vs 36.6, K=41/42 3.6 vs 27.7 -> planner extrapolation FAILED (~7x pessimistic) although correctly labelled \"extrapolated\". Fix: decode model from bytes/bandwidth (host constant) instead of Granite-fitted per-layer time (delegated).\nClassification: runtime-placement trade-off (existing engine features) on proxy-2; the factory's baseline frontier for this model.\n# D040 — release-1 speed qualified; PAP pap12_q3 provisional accept RETRACTED after quiet-host rerun\n(b) Job job-85805b616c (release-1, 24/24): Q8_0 tg128 OFF 793.2 [792.8-793.6] ON 810.4 [810.2-810.6] = +2.17%; mixE4K 704.3 -> 717.0 (+1.8%); completion decode Q8_0 763.0 -> 779.1 (+2.1%). q8_reuse gain survives stacking with kv_quant_fix. release-1 = QUALIFIED (correctness D037 + speed).\n(c) Job job-d2e342e743 (b0, 5 alternating rounds, quiet host): mixE4K_n12 213.1 [208.9-216.8], mix4_imat_n12 208.9 [206.3-215.8], pap12_q3_n12 216.3 [214.7-234.9] => +1.5% (< +5% rule) -> pap12_q3 REJECTED; D035's +9.8% was download-period noise (D035 flagged it provisional). mixE4K_n24 130.0 vs pap24_q3_n24 145.3 (+11.8%) but KLD 0.117 fails quality.\nWhy smaller than the bandwidth model's ~+15% at K=12: Q3_K unpacking costs more CPU compute per byte on AVX2 than Q4_K, so fewer bits != proportionally faster on CPU. Factory rule: screen per-quant-type CPU expert throughput (CPU-only llama-bench per type) before choosing CPU-side precision; imatrix (D035) remains accepted (quality, not speed).\nMethod lesson confirmed: provisional results from noisy windows must be re-run before acceptance; the rerun reversed one.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
